  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There is a marginal risk of excessive \nrainfall today/tonight. \n\nA very moist airmass will remain in place today with PWATs \nranging from 1.75\" to 2.00\". Surface front and associated upper\nlevel trough which helped focus the showers/storms the last \ncouple of days/nights is now weakening with little in the way of\ndynamic forcing remaining. From this point forward shower \nactivity will depend mainly on thermodynamic support with \ndaytime heating contributing to the convective cycle today. CAMS\nsuggest low level lift will be primarily orographic with \nafternoon deep convection firing along the ridgelines of both \nthe Blue Ridge and the Highlands-Alleghany Mountains of WV. As \nsuch, think these will be the geographic areas to watch for the \nheaviest rain, but nothing organized enough to warrant a flood \nwatch. Will continue to handle the localized frog stranglers \nwith short fused flood advisories if needed. Unless there is \nstorm cell training think any flash flooding would be localized\ndue to the faster cell movement today...mean winds increasing.\n\nTemperatures today should be similar to yesterday with highs in\nthe 80s. It will remain muggy with dewpoints near 70. Wind flow\nthis afternoon will be from the south, mean wind from south- \nnorth at about 14-18 mph, yesteday's easterly component no \nlonger evident in any of the forecast soundings.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will persist through the rest\nof the week and weekend with daily chances of showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nForecast area will remain wedged between a large upper level \nridge over Bermuda and an upper level trough to our northwest. \nWind flow between these two features will maintain a persistent\nflow of moisture into the forecast area, enough to fuel daily \nshowers and thunderstorms, most of which should coincide with \nthe peak heating of the day favoring the afternoon and evening \nhours. We are not expecting anything severe, but a marginal risk\nfor flash flooding will continue to be a hazard due to the \npotential for high rain rates which may lead to runoff issues.",
